<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">National Convention</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1792">1792</a>–<a href="/years/1795">1795</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as French National Convention</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The National Convention was the elected assembly that governed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> from September 1792 to October 1795. On its first full day it abolished the monarchy and proclaimed the French Republic, and in 1793 it tried and executed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xvi">Louis XVI</a>. Under pressure of war and revolt it gave power to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/committee-of-public-safety">Committee of Public Safety</a>, which ran the Terror, and after Robespierre's fall it drafted the constitution of the Directory.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/national-convention.jpg" alt="National Convention" width="320" height="165" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Fluffy89502 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:French_National_Convention_Composition_Pre-purge.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The National Convention was created after the insurrection of 10 August 1792. The Legislative Assembly decreed the provisional suspension of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xvi">Louis XVI</a> and called a convention to draw up a constitution with no monarchy. Its deputies were to be elected by all Frenchmen aged 21 or older, domiciled for a year and living by the product of their labour, so that it was the first French assembly elected by a suffrage without distinctions of class. The indirect election took place from 2 to 10 September 1792, with a turnout of 11.9 per cent. The Convention had 749 deputies, not counting 33 from the colonies, and lawyers were the most numerous. It first sat on 20 September 1792.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Three factions divided the Convention: the Montagnards or Mountain, the Marais or Plain, and the Girondins, also called Brissotins. Historians are divided on the makeup, but the Mountain was the largest, with around 302 to 309 deputies, against 178 to 227 Girondins and 153 to 250 in the Plain. On the fate of the king, most Montagnards favoured judgment and execution, while the Girondins were divided. Opinion turned against Louis on 20 November 1792 after the discovery of a cache of 726 documents. Military setbacks and the war in the Vendée, which began in March 1793, were used to portray the Girondins as soft, and the insurrection of 31 May to 2 June 1793 brought their fall.</p><p>From April 1793 power was delegated to the small <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/committee-of-public-safety">Committee of Public Safety</a>. After armed sections again surrounded the Convention on 5 September 1793, it put the Terror on the agenda and created a revolutionary army. The Committee then eliminated the Hébertists and the Dantonists, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georges-danton">Georges Danton</a> and Camille Desmoulins. Many deputies who had backed the Committee no longer supported it by mid-1794. After Robespierre's fall the Convention sat for another year, until a new constitution was written and the Directory began on 26 October 1795.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>By a decree of 4 February 1794, after speeches by deputies from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-domingue">Saint-Domingue</a>, the Convention extended to the whole <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-colonial-empire">French colonial empire</a> the abolition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> that the commissioners <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leger-felicite-sonthonax">Léger-Félicité Sonthonax</a> and Étienne Polverel had proclaimed in Saint-Domingue in 1793. It did not affect Martinique or Guadeloupe, and the law of 20 May 1802 abolished the decree. The historian Anchel concluded in 1911 that the Convention had saved <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> from civil war and invasion and founded a system of public education, including the École Polytechnique and the École Normale Supérieure, and that it created the Grand Livre de la Dette publique.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “National Convention” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Delegates at the <b>National Convention</b> of 1908–1909, among them the Cape prime minister John X. Merriman, agreed a constitution that kept the Cape’s non-racial franchise within the province but allowed only white men to sit in the Union parliament.</blockquote><a href="/empire/cape-colony">Cape Colony · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>A committee of six members of the Legislative Assembly oversaw elections to a <b>National Convention</b>, charged with abolishing the monarchy and drafting a new constitution.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-first-republic">French First Republic · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>War with Austria from April 1792 and fear of betrayal led to the storming of the Tuileries on 10 August 1792; the <b>National Convention</b> abolished the monarchy on 21 September 1792, and Louis XVI was guillotined in January 1793.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-france">Kingdom of France · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>On 1 October 1795 the <b>National Convention</b> annexed the principality to France, dividing it chiefly between the departments of Ourthe and Meuse-Inférieure.</blockquote><a href="/empire/prince-bishopric-of-liege">Prince-Bishopric of Liège · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>National Convention</b>'s decree of 15 May 1792 restored the rights of the free people of colour; Léger-Félicité Sonthonax, Civil Commissioner and de facto ruler from September 1792 to 1795, stripped the white Creoles of military power and abolished slavery.</blockquote><a href="/empire/saint-domingue">Saint-Domingue · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Rivalry among the four colonies over railways and customs duties, together with a wish among white politicians to settle ‘native policy’ on a common footing, led to the <b>National Convention</b> of 1908–1909, which met in Durban, Cape Town and Bloemfontein.</blockquote><a href="/empire/union-of-south-africa">Union of South Africa · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
